 and STRONG exclusion of chronic disease exacerbation (B5). No formal clinical…"}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Does the acute/septal pain trajectory (progressive dull discomfort becoming sharp with exercise) align with typical patterns of chronic liver disease deterioration rather than sudden ischemic event?", "summary": "The findings of subacute RUQ pain progressing to sudden exacerbation with exercise and absence of fever/jaundice provide MODERATE support for hepatic vascular occlusion (B2), WEAK reduction for gynecologic emergency (B1), and STRONG exclusion of chronic disease exacerbation (B5). No formal clinical…"}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "How does the patient's recent rapid weight loss for a fitness show and heavy alcohol use impact the plausibility of gynecologic emergency as the hemodynamic etiology?", "summary": "The findings of subacute RUQ pain progressing to sudden exacerbation with exercise and absence of fever/jaundice provide MODERATE support for hepatic vascular occlusion (B2), WEAK reduction for gynecologic emergency (B1), and STRONG exclusion of chronic disease exacerbation (B5). No formal clinical…"}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Are the physical findings of burly shoulders, thick neck, and acne (consistent with PCOS/androgen excess) more plausible as unrelated coincidences or as evidence against hepatic vascular occlusion?", "summary": "The findings of subacute RUQ pain progressing to sudden exacerbation with exercise and absence of fever/jaundice provide MODERATE support for hepatic vascular occlusion (B2), WEAK reduction for gynecologic emergency (B1), and STRONG exclusion of chronic disease exacerbation (B5). No formal clinical…"}, {"t": 2, "type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "content": "Would the sudden worsening of pain during exercise suggest an acute complication rather than exacerbation of a chronic disease like alcoholic hepatitis?", "summary": "The findings of subacute RUQ pain progressing to sudden exacerbation with exercise and absence of fever/jaundice provide MODERATE support for hepatic vascular occlusion (B2), WEAK reduction for gynecologic emergency (B1), and STRONG exclusion of chronic disease exacerbation (B5). No formal clinical…"}], "differential_history": [{"top": [["B2", 0.429], ["B3", 0.343], ["B1", 0.152]]}, {"top": [["B2", 0.581], ["B3", 0.258], ["B2.1", 0.258]]}], "deliberation": {}, "interrupt": {"active": true, "reason": "Hemodynamic instability with hypotension (80/40 mmHg) and tachycardia (110 bpm) represents a major haemodynamic emergency requiring stabilization before differential diagnosis resolution.", "required_actions": ["Initiate vasopressors for shock management", "Confirm lactate/acid-base status", "Expand IV fluid resuscitation", "Order emergent ultrasound (e.g., FAST exam) for source localization"]}, "termination": {"ready_to_stop": false, "termination_type": "continue", "reason": "Active emergency interrupt requires urgent shock management, but info_exhaustion not yet met. B2 (hepatic vascular occlusion) posterior (58%) fails confirmation threshold and dangerous alternatives (B1 - 11.5% posterior) remain active. No actionable_parent consensus across frontier branches. Next diagnostic step should resolve β-hCG pending results and prioritize further discriminators for B2.1 (Budd-Chiari syndrome)."}, "turn_budget_used": 2, "estimated_remaining_value": 0.7, "max_turn_budget": 5, "latest_action_type": "ANALYZE_VIGNETTE", "diagnosis_readiness_score": 0.25806451612903225, "benchmark_output_ready": false, "static_question": "Which of the following is the most likely the diagnosis?", "static_options": [{"id": "A", "description": "Ectopic implantation of a blastocyst"}, {"id": "B", "description": "Inflammation of the pancreas"}, {"id": "C", "description": "Obstruction of blood flow through the hepatic vein"}, {"id": "D", "description": "Obstruction of the common bile duct by radio-opaque stones"}, {"id": "E", "description": "Vascular ectasia within the liver"}], "seen_evidence_ids": [], "seen_evidence_phenotypes": "{'Intrahepatic cholestasis with episodic jaundice', 'pain', 'alcoholic hepatitis', 'hepatitis', 'Cholestatic liver disease'}", "mode_policy": {"benchmark_purity": true, "allow_external_knowledge": true}, "answer_option_mapping": {}, "tool_use_log": [], "root_revision_needed": false, "max_tree_depth": 3, "reasoning_ledger": {"anchor": {"hypothesis": "B2", "t": 0, "posterior_at_anchor": 0.429}, "leader": {"branch_id": "B3", "label": "Acute Biliary Tract Disease Including Choledocholithiasis", "posterior": 0.258, "leading_since_t": 0, "n_revisions": 0}, "leader_evidence": {"confirming": 3, "disconfirming": 0, "last_disconfirming_digest": ""}, "action_intents": [{"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}, {"t": 2, "intent": "broaden"}], "considered_alternatives": ["B2.1", "B2.2", "B2.3"]}, "discriminator_hints": "[Knowledge Layer: coverage=50%, source=both]\n\nAcute Biliary Tract Disease Including Choledocholithiasis vs Budd-Chiari Syndrome (Idiopathic or Hypercoagulability-Driven):\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: abdominal pain, acute hepatic failure, budd-chiari syndrome, cholecystitis, cirrhosis\n\nAcute Biliary Tract Disease Including Choledocholithiasis vs Septic Hepatic Vein Thrombosis (Post-Gonococcal Embolic Complication):\n  Favours first:  2-3 toe syndactyly, abdominal distention, abnormal astrocyte morphology, abnormal brainstem mri signal intensity, abnormal epiphyseal ossification\n  Favours second: abducens palsy, abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal circulating copper concentration, abnormal cranial nerve morphology\n\nBudd-Chiari Syndrome (Idiopathic or Hypercoagulability-Driven) vs Septic Hepatic Vein Thrombosis (Post-Gonococcal Embolic Complication):\n  Favours first:  abdominal pain, acute hepatic failure, ascites, budd-chiari syndrome, cholecystitis\n  Favours second: abducens palsy, abnormal auditory evoked potentials, abnormal cerebral white matter morphology, abnormal circulating copper concentration, abnormal cranial nerve morphology\n\nRelated to Acute Biliary Tract Disease Including Choledocholithiasis: bile duct disease, bile reflux, biliary tract neoplasm (disease)\n\nRelated to Budd-Chiari Syndrome (Idiopathic or Hypercoagulability-Driven): hepatic vascular disease\n\nRelated to Septic Hepatic Vein Thrombosis (Post-Gonococcal Embolic Complication): hepatic vascular disease\n\n[Indirect reasoning chains (PrimeKG 2-hop):]\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → abdominal distention → Acute Biliary Tract Disease Including Choledocholithiasis\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → arthralgia → Acute Biliary Tract Disease Including Choledocholithiasis\n  A 23-year-old woman presents to the emergency department with severe abdominal pain → shoulder pain → Septic Hepatic Vein Thrombosis (Post-Gonococcal Embolic Complication)"}
